column1:
Real code:

// SnakeGame - the public entry points
public void move(Direction direction) { state.move(this, direction); }
public void pause()  { state.pause(this); }
public void resume() { state.resume(this); }

public void setState(IGameState state) { this.state = state; }

public boolean isOver() { return state == GameOverState.getInstance(); }

These are the only ways the outside world can act on a game, and none of them contains an if/else about whether the game is running, paused or finished. Where does that decision get made?,Nowhere - all three are always allowed,In whichever IGameState object currently sits in the 'state' field: it decides what each call means and whether it is permitted at all,"In Main, which checks isOver() before every call","In the Board, which rejects moves once the game ends",,2,Replacing the object in the 'state' field changes the behaviour of all three entry points at once. That is why none of them needs to branch.,2

Real code:

public class RunningState implements IGameState {

    @Override
    public void move(SnakeGame game, Direction direction) {
        game.performMove(direction);
    }

    @Override
    public void pause(SnakeGame game) {
        game.setState(PausedState.getInstance());
    }

    @Override
    public void resume(SnakeGame game) {
        throw new InvalidMoveException("game is already running");
    }
}

public class PausedState implements IGameState {

    @Override
    public void move(SnakeGame game, Direction direction) {
        throw new InvalidMoveException("cannot move while paused");
    }

    @Override
    public void pause(SnakeGame game) {
        throw new InvalidMoveException("game is already paused");
    }

    @Override
    public void resume(SnakeGame game) {
        game.setState(RunningState.getInstance());
    }
}

The game is running normally. Main calls game.pause() and then immediately game.move(Direction.UP). Trace both calls: what happens?,Both succeed

Real code:

public class GameOverState implements IGameState {

    @Override
    public void move(SnakeGame game, Direction direction) {
        throw new InvalidMoveException("game is over");
    }

    @Override
    public void pause(SnakeGame game) {
        throw new InvalidMoveException("game is over");
    }

    @Override
    public void resume(SnakeGame game) {
        throw new InvalidMoveException("game is over");
    }
}

Every method in GameOverState throws. Compare that with PausedState, where resume() is allowed. What does this difference say about the two conditions?,Nothing - the difference is accidental,"Paused is a temporary condition with a defined way out, so exactly one operation is legal. Game over is terminal, so no operation can be legal - encoding that as three throws makes the finality impossible to bypass",GameOverState should also allow resume() so a player can retry,PausedState should also throw on resume() for consistency,,2,"The requirement said 'over must stay over'. Because there is no non-throwing path out of GameOverState, that rule is enforced by the type rather than by everyone remembering to check isOver() first.",2

