All three states are singletons with private constructors and no fields of their own. Suppose a new PausedState needed to remember how many moves had elapsed before the pause. What breaks?,Nothing - the singleton can hold a counter,"Two games sharing the one PausedState instance would share that counter, so pausing one game would corrupt the other's value. The state would have to stop being a singleton, or the counter would have to live on SnakeGame",The private constructor would prevent the field from being initialised,Only the first game created would be able to pause,,2,Field-less states can be shared safely

Real code:

private void endGame() {
    setState(GameOverState.getInstance());
    notifyGameOver();
}

endGame() is private and is the only place in SnakeGame that sets GameOverState. What does routing every ending through this one method guarantee?,That the game can only end once,"That the state change and the observer notification can never happen without each other - no future code path can end the game and forget to tell the listeners, or notify listeners without actually ending it",That the score is reset to zero,That endGame() runs faster than two separate calls,,2,"performMove() has two separate ways to finish - a wall and a self-collision. Both call endGame(), so neither can drift out of step with the other.",2

Real code:

public void performMove(Direction direction) {
    Point currentHead = body.peekFirst();
    Point rawNewHead = new Point(currentHead.getRow() + direction.getDeltaRow(),
            currentHead.getCol() + direction.getDeltaCol());

    Point newHead = handleBoundary(rawNewHead);   // <-- the one varying step
    if (newHead == null) {
        endGame();
        return;
    }

    Point tail = body.peekLast();
    boolean eatsFood = newHead.equals(currentFood.getPosition());
    boolean bitesItself = occupied.contains(newHead) && !(newHead.equals(tail));

    if (bitesItself) {
        endGame();
        return;
    }

    if (eatsFood) {
        body.addFirst(newHead);
        occupied.add(newHead);
        score += scoringRule.pointsFor(currentFood);
        currentFood = spawnFood();
        notifyScoreChanged();
    } else {
        body.pollLast();
        occupied.remove(tail);
        body.addFirst(newHead);
        occupied.add(newHead);
    }
}

protected abstract Point handleBoundary(Point rawNewHead);

performMove() is public, yet the design intends it to be reached only through RunningState. What is the practical risk of that being public, and why was it done anyway?,No risk - public is always safe for game logic,A caller could invoke performMove() directly and bypass the pause and game-over checks entirely. It is public because RunningState lives in a different package (engine.state) and could not otherwise call it,It is public so that Main can display the snake,There is no risk because performMove() checks isOver() internally,,2,"A genuine trade-off forced by the package split. Same shape as VendingMachine.setState() being public - the state classes need access, and Java's package-private visibility cannot express 'only my state classes'.",2

