Suppose the three state classes were replaced by 'private GameStatus status;' - an enum - plus a switch inside move(), pause() and resume(). The program's behaviour today would be identical. What actually gets worse?,Nothing measurable - three conditions in one switch is arguably easier to read at a glance,"Everything Paused does would be split across three different switch statements instead of sitting in one class, and adding a fourth condition means editing three working methods","It would not compile, because Java cannot switch on enums",The game could no longer change condition at runtime,,2,"Worth conceding in class: at three conditions the enum is genuinely readable. The cost appears on the fourth condition, and whenever you want to read one condition's full behaviour in one place.",2

