In the real run of Main, the greedy bot ended the game and the next line printed 'expected failure: game is over'. Which call produced that message, and why did it not simply return?,move() returned false and Main printed the message itself,"A call made after the game had ended reached GameOverState, which threw InvalidMoveException - Main catches it and prints the message, so the failure is visible rather than silent",Main checked isOver() and printed the message without calling anything,The board threw an out-of-bounds exception,,2,The observable proof that the terminal condition is enforced: the game did not quietly accept a move on a finished game and pretend nothing was wrong.,1

Real code:

protected SnakeGame(Board board, Point startPosition,
                    IFoodSpawnStrategy foodSpawnStrategy, Random random) {
    this.board = board;
    this.foodSpawnStrategy = foodSpawnStrategy;
    this.random = random;
    body.addFirst(startPosition);
    occupied.add(startPosition);
    this.currentFood = spawnFood();
}

// SnakeGame - the public entry points
public void move(Direction direction) { state.move(this, direction); }
public void pause()  { state.pause(this); }
public void resume() { state.resume(this); }

public void setState(IGameState state) { this.state = state; }

public boolean isOver() { return state == GameOverState.getInstance(); }

A new SnakeGame begins in RunningState, decided by the field initialiser 'private IGameState state = RunningState.getInstance();'. What would need to change for games to start paused, waiting for the player to press start?,Every state class would need a new startGame() method,"Only the initial value - set the field to PausedState.getInstance() so the first legal call is resume(). No other class changes, because pause and resume already exist",SnakeGame would need a boolean started flag checked in move(),A new StartingState class is unavoidable,,2,A nice demonstration that the conditions already form a complete little machine: changing where you enter it is a one-word change.,1

